00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:01.240 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  I'm here on the beautiful island man in  the middle of the Irish Sea and today  we're going to explore the science and  waterwheels  and behind me is the 22-metre to Lexi  wheel the largest working waterwheel in  the world  [Music]  this wheel is over a hundred and sixty  years old and was built at the height of  the Industrial Revolution was a way to  pump water out of the lac C Line shops  [Music]  [Applause]  this mine was an integral part of the  islands economy that is Pete it employed  up to six hundred people and reduce  twenty percent of the British output of  lead zinc and copper and the trouble  with digging deep holes in the ground is  there any water likes to run in and fill  a hole up and so the lines engineered to  find a way to keep pumping all of this  water out and they've certainly done a  lot here the vertical mine shafts from  here are almost 500 metres deep so I  imagine the amount of energy required to  continually keep pumping all that heavy  water out so why can all that energy  come from  there wasn't any chrome available on the  island however one thing they do have an  abundance of here in the Isle of Man is  running water and so they built this  huge machine to harness all that  hydrological power I have to say I do  find something rather ingenious by  solving the problem of excess water by  using more water water from local  streams are fed into a pipe that leads  into the top of the wheel the water then  falls from a pipe into the buckets this  makes the wheel rotate in what is  described as a reverse direction known  as a back shot wheel this crank tells  the circular motion of the wheel into  horizontal motion and as it completes a  revolution the rod moves back and forth  a total of 2.5 metres this horizontal  motion is then turned into vertical  motion down into the mine shafts so from  a physics perspective this wheel is  simply a machine that takes the  gravitational potential energy in the  falling water turns it into rotational  energy and then into more useful  straight-line kinetic or mechanical  energy when it comes to machines though  it's more useful to think of them in  terms of their power the power is just  the measurement of the rate at which a  machine does its work how much energy it  uses per second same as you would  calculate the speed of a car in terms of  its distance travelled per hour you  calculate the power of a machine by  dividing the change in energy by time  the standard unit of power is known as a  what however for large machines like  this they usually refer to in terms of  horsepower  one metric horsepower unit is defined as  the power required to raise a mass of 75  kilograms of one meter vertically off  the ground in one second which equals  735 watts and the lack C wheel is  estimated to put out around 180 horse  powers when she's in full swing and this  is the formula to calculate the force  power of a waterwheel I know it looks  stressful but it's actually surprisingly  simple because the only operation you're  using is multiplication so this means  that if you increase or decrease one of  the factors then the power increases or  decreases proportionately the head is  the distance the water will fall the  flow is the volume of water the wheel  will capture and gravity is the  acceleration due to gravity the  efficiency of a water wheel is the  measurement of how much of its input  energy is successfully converted into  alpha energy and the light see wheels  Epson design means it's over 80 percent  efficient so just why is the waxy wheel  so damn big well as a formula before  showed the greater the distance the  water has to fall the more power is  generated and so the bigger the diameter  of the wheel the more gravitational  potential energy there is to capture  from the water so I guess the saying is  true bigger is better  modern hydroelectric dams like the  Hoover Dam are arguably the giant  descendants  water will because they to take  advantage of the movement of water  flowing downhill dams are an enormous  storage of waters gravitational  potential energy which is then captured  as it channels through turbines which  spin and generate electricity today  hydroelectric power provides almost  seventeen percent of the world's  electricity supply which is great news  because it's a very sustainable and non  polluting source of energy and to think  it all started out with a humble  waterwheel  thank you will

